mk(1, 'Werner theory', 'In Werner&rsquo;s theory, the primary valency of a metal in a complex corresponds to its', 'oxidation state, satisfied by ionisable (negative) ions', ['coordination number, satisfied by neutral molecules only', 'number of d electrons', 'number of lone pairs on the metal'], '<p>Primary valency is ionisable and equals the oxidation state; it is satisfied by negative ions (e.g. Cl<sup>-</sup>). Secondary valency is non-ionisable and equals the coordination number; it is satisfied by ligands, whether neutral or anionic, and has a fixed direction in space.</p>');
mk(1, 'Werner theory', 'Secondary valency in Werner&rsquo;s theory is the same as the modern', 'coordination number', ['oxidation number', 'atomic number', 'denticity'], '<p>Secondary valency = number of ligand donor atoms bound to the metal = coordination number. It is non-ionisable and directional.</p>');
